## Summary

The task required that the **effective** registration date always fall on a business day: any weekend date — whether supplied in the request or defaulted to the server date — rolls forward to the next Monday, and every value derived from the registration date must use the adjusted value.

I implemented this at the single point where the effective registration date is established, in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):

```java
LocalDate effectiveRegistrationDate =
    owner.getRegistrationDate() != null ? owner.getRegistrationDate() : LocalDate.now();
owner.setRegistrationDate(toBusinessDay(effectiveRegistrationDate));
```

plus a helper:

```java
private static LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate date) {
    return switch (date.getDayOfWeek()) {
        case SATURDAY -> date.plusDays(2);
        case SUNDAY -> date.plusDays(1);
        default -> date;
    };
}
```

Why this location satisfies all the requirements:

- **Supplied vs. defaulted** — both branches feed through `toBusinessDay`, so a client-supplied weekend date now rolls forward too (the behaviour change called out in the spec).
- **Derived values (membership number)** — `OwnerMapper.membershipNumber` reads `owner.getRegistrationDate()`, which is now the adjusted date before the DTO is mapped, so the year segment (`-M<YY>`) uses the adjusted date.
- **Daily create-limit** — `ClinicServiceImpl.rejectWhenDailyLimitReached` counts existing owners by `registrationDate`. Since every owner is created through this endpoint with its date already rolled forward, the count is naturally per adjusted business day.

Only the V1 controller creates owners (V2 only lists/pages), so no other code path needed changing. The task specified weekend→Monday only, so I did not add holiday handling (no test requires it and the spec doesn't mention it).

